that the Board has violated the Center’s organic statutes and its fiduciary duties as a board of

trustees. Following the Board’s reconsideration (twice) of the question with new evidence before

it and the adoption of a superseding closure resolution, neither theory has merit. And Plaintiff’s

apparent primary basis for opposing summary judgment now—the contention that the Board’s

closure vote was a pretext to hide the Center’s “deteriorating” finances—fails both because it is



